{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 40, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "ושמרת את חוקיו ואת מצותיו", :translit "u'shemarta et chukav v'et mitzvotav", :gloss "and you shall keep His statutes and His commandments"} {:he "לאז יבדיל", :translit "la'az yavdil", :gloss "[he] joined to"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "S'mikhah: the verse's syntactic juxtaposition of keeping commandments with the result 'that it may go well with you' teaches that observance produces atonement"}], :frame {:text "The placement of a commandment immediately adjacent to its stated consequence allows the reader to derive a causal principle from the juxtaposition (s'mikhut ha'parshiyot). Here, the reward of well-being promised after 'you shall keep His statutes' (Deuteronomy 4:40) is read as flowing from an intermediate effect: the atonement generated by Torah study and mitzvah observance. The principle that Torah study and performance of commandments produce atonement (kaparah) is discussed in multiple rabbinic sources, including Sotah 21a regarding Torah study protecting from suffering, Yoma 86a regarding teshuvah and mitzvot as atonement, and Leviticus Rabbah 7:3 on the purifying power of Torah and deeds.", :sources ["Deuteronomy 4:40" "Sotah 21a" "Yoma 86a" "Leviticus Rabbah 7:3"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :other, :note "S'mikhah: the verse's syntactic juxtaposition of keeping commandments with the result 'that it may go well with you' teaches that observance produces atonement", :by :selah, :ran? false}], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 4, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:34:10.903847079Z", :model "glm-5.2"}